WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.730
Our rain report said Riverside had more

2
00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:05.050
than two hundred millimetres in a week.

3
00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:07.560
The river was nearly dry!

4
00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:10.660
One gauge had sent nine nine

5
00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:12.100
nine, its error code.

6
00:00:13.330 --> 00:00:16.290
Today: blanks, zeros and repeats,

7
00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:18.230
and the checks that catch them.

8
00:00:19.390 --> 00:00:21.230
A blank is not a zero.

9
00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:23.900
Zero rain means the gauge was

10
00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:25.280
read, and it was dry.

11
00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:28.470
A blank means nobody read it.

12
00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:31.120
And for a pupil's mark, a blank

13
00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:33.600
might mean absent, not yet

14
00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:35.540
marked, or simply forgotten.

15
00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:38.870
So never fill a blank silently.

16
00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:41.620
Count it as zero, and the average

17
00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:44.240
drops; count it as present, and

18
00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:45.960
attendance looks better than it was.

19
00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:48.740
Leave it out, count how many,

20
00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:50.460
and say so in your report.

21
00:00:51.730 --> 00:00:54.950
Repeats are rows typed twice: the

22
00:00:54.950 --> 00:00:56.810
same pupil, on the same day.

23
00:00:57.410 --> 00:00:58.790
But they only show up

24
00:00:58.790 --> 00:00:59.990
once the names agree.

25
00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:05.120
Amina on Monday and Aminah on Monday look

26
00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:08.160
like two pupils until you clean the names.

27
00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:12.380
A check is a rule every row must pass.

28
00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:15.780
In range: rain from zero to

29
00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:17.380
three hundred millimetres.

30
00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:18.700
Not empty.

31
00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:20.120
No repeats.

32
00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:23.040
And only names that are on the list.

33
00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:26.350
When a check fails, you decide.

34
00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:29.550
Stop the whole batch, set the bad rows

35
00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:32.470
aside, or let them through with a warning.

36
00:01:33.170 --> 00:01:35.510
Either way, write it in your cleaning log:

37
00:01:36.010 --> 00:01:38.770
what, where, what you did, and why.

38
00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:40.560
Quick check!

39
00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:43.850
One pupil's mark is blank.

40
00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:46.930
When I work out the class average,

41
00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:48.690
what should I do with it?

42
00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:51.530
Leave it out, and say that

43
00:01:51.530 --> 00:01:52.710
one mark is missing.

44
00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:55.460
Counting it as zero pulls the average

45
00:01:55.460 --> 00:01:57.760
down, and filling in the average

46
00:01:57.760 --> 00:01:59.600
invents a mark nobody earned.

47
00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:03.260
Python can check every row for you.

48
00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:06.750
To find repeats, keep a set of what you've

49
00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:09.670
seen: the station and the day together.

50
00:02:10.430 --> 00:02:11.670
If a row's pair is already

51
00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:13.170
there, it's a repeat.

52
00:02:14.490 --> 00:02:17.030
In the app, four weather clubs send

53
00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:18.770
rain readings every evening.

54
00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:21.730
Turn the checks on and off, and

55
00:02:21.730 --> 00:02:23.550
choose what happens when one fails.

56
00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:26.840
With every check off, the report shows

57
00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:28.940
six stations instead of four.

58
00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:30.680
Your turn.

59
00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:33.170
A batch has one bad reading

60
00:02:33.170 --> 00:02:34.750
among twenty good ones.

61
00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:37.920
Which choice keeps the good data moving?

62
00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:41.540
Set the bad row aside, and send

63
00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:42.900
it back to the club to fix.

64
00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:46.400
Remember: a blank is not a zero,

65
00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:48.440
so never fill it silently.

66
00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:50.870
Clean the names before you

67
00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:51.490
look for repeats.

68
00:02:52.310 --> 00:02:54.410
And check every batch, and

69
00:02:54.410 --> 00:02:55.430
log what you changed.

70
00:02:56.740 --> 00:02:57.960
Try it in class tomorrow:

71
00:02:58.200 --> 00:02:59.940
play “Data detective”.

72
00:03:00.410 --> 00:03:03.390
Give groups a paper rain table with four

73
00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:06.570
hidden problems: a blank, a nine nine

74
00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:09.210
nine, a minus sign and a repeat.

75
00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:12.420
They find them, decide what to do,

76
00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:14.220
and write a cleaning log.
